1 How to draw 1000 ages like the group

Write down every age in the group and how many students have that age. For example: age 19 has 2 students, age 20 has 5 students, and so on.
Add the counts up as you go: 2, 7, 14, 19, 22, 24, 25. The last number is the total number of students.
Pick a random number from 1 to 25.
Look through the list and stop at the first number that is as big as your random number or bigger. The age next to it is your answer.
Do this 1000 times and count how many times each age came out.

An age with more students gets more of the random numbers, so it comes out more often. That makes the 1000 ages look like the real group.


2 Why can’t we make a Singleton with new from outside?

A Singleton is a class where only one object is allowed. To make sure of that, we make its constructor private. A private constructor
 can only be used inside the class itself. So if someone outside writes new Clock(), Java gives an error. The only way to get the object
 is getInstance(). The first time, it makes the object. After that, it gives back the same one every time.

 3 Distributions we can use in the simulator

 Uniform: every number in a range has the same chance. Example: picking a random choice out of equal options.
 Exponential: used for the waiting time between things that happen randomly. Example: the time between two customers arriving.
 Normal: most values are near the average, and very few are far away. Example: how tall people are, or how long a service takes.
 Poisson: how many things happen in a fixed time. Example: how many customers arrive in one hour.
 Triangular: when you only know the smallest value, the most common value and the biggest value.
